Transfer unidirectional byte streams between processes using anonymous pipes (pipe) and named pipes (mkfifo) in the filesystem.

What you''ll learn
- Anonymous Kernel Pipes
- Named Pipes (FIFOs)

Subtopics
Anonymous Kernel Pipes: Creating pipe file descriptors and redirecting child stdout to parent stdin.
Named Pipes (FIFOs): Creating persistent filesystem nodes that allow unrelated processes to stream data.

Learning outcome
Understand unix pipes and named fifos, complete the practical exercise, and explain the key concepts.

Tools and setup
Use a free editor (such as VS Code), modern web browser, and relevant local runtime/CLI; no paid service or subscription is required for these exercises.

Practice
Write a C program where a parent process creates a pipe, forks a child, and sends a message through the pipe.

