Tune kernel network buffers, virtual memory swappiness, and file descriptor limits dynamically via sysctl and /etc/sysctl.conf.

What you''ll learn
- Virtual Memory Tunables
- Network Stack Tunables

Subtopics
Virtual Memory Tunables: Tuning vm.swappiness, vm.dirty_ratio, and vm.overcommit_memory for server workloads.
Network Stack Tunables: Increasing net.core.somaxconn and tcp_tw_reuse to handle high-concurrency web traffic.

Learning outcome
Understand kernel parameter tuning with sysctl, complete the practical exercise, and explain the key concepts.

Tools and setup
Use a free editor (such as VS Code), modern web browser, and relevant local runtime/CLI; no paid service or subscription is required for these exercises.

Practice
Tune a kernel parameter using sysctl -w and persist the setting across reboots in /etc/sysctl.d/99-custom.conf.

Completion check
You can show and explain the outcome of: tune a kernel parameter using sysctl -w and persist the setting across reboots in /etc/sysctl.d/99-custom.conf. Check that it functions as expected and meets requirements.', 0) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
